## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Context memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` plus type-specific slots:

- **`sandboxed`** (required) — boolean.  Most-important authorization predicate.  Sandboxed contexts have stricter firewall and reduced corpus access
- **`host`** — named host identifier.  Distinct from `sandboxed?` — a single host can run both sandboxed and unsandboxed contexts
- **`firewall-class`** — categorical regime: `none` / `proprietary-tokens` / `private-project` / ...
- **`authorization-scope`** — natural-language description of what an actor in this context is authorized to read, write, modify

{{slot-table}}

## Firewall-class vocabulary

- **`none`** — no firewall (current default for unsandboxed authoring contexts)
- **`proprietary-tokens`** — proprietary-token filtering applies (catches accidental leaks of engagement-specific identifiers)
- **`private-project`** — full sandboxed private-project enforcement; both proprietary tokens AND path restrictions

## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What runtime this context names

<Concrete description of the environment: hardware, sandboxing,
network access, filesystem reach.>

## Authorization scope

<Natural-language description of what's permitted here.  Cite
relevant authorization memorials.>

## Firewall regime

<What filters apply; what tokens / paths are blocked; what
sanitization fires.>

## Actor instances operating in this context

<Cross-references to actor memorials that runs-in-context here.>

## See also

<Adjacent contexts, governing authorizations, applicable rules.>
```

## Identity-and-context orthogonality

A single actor identity may operate in multiple contexts; the substrate distinguishes via the multi-arity `runs-in-context:` edge rather than per-context actor instances.  This is the ContextL-style `:in-layer` dispatch pattern — orthogonal identity and context axes that compose at rule-firing time.

## Pitfalls

- **Per-context actor proliferation** — one actor + multi-arity `runs-in-context:` is correct; separate per-context actor instances are an anti-pattern
- **Missing `sandboxed`** — the single most load-bearing field; never omit
- **Vague `authorization-scope`** — should be concrete enough that audit can consult it; *"do appropriate things"* is not useful

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`actor`** — actors reference contexts via `runs-in-context:` (multi-arity)
- **`rule`** — rules dispatch on context as part of the (actor, context, event) tuple
- **`event`** — events may carry context-specific applicability constraints
- **`authorization`** — authorizations may scope to specific contexts

